"There are various views regarding the letters Yā-Sīn at the beginning of the surah. According to the majority, this is the name of the surah. According to some, it is an oath, and according to others, it is among the names of Allah. According to one narration, it is one of the names of the Prophet (peace be upon him) and means \"O human\" or \"O master.\" Following these letters, the expression \"By the wise Qur'an\" emphasizes the truth of the Prophet's messengerhood. Such powerful affirmations are intended to console and reassure the Prophet in the face of the disbelievers' fierce opposition.\n\nIt is stated that the Prophet is on a straight path, and that this path is the Sharia of Islam. The purpose of the Qur'an's revelation is to warn a people whose forefathers were not warned and who are in heedlessness. This people is the Arab society, because no prophet had been sent to them before. The Qur'an addresses both all of humanity and specifically the Arabs.\n\nThe expression \"Already has the word of punishment proven true against most of them\" announces that, like previous communities, most of this people will not believe. This word of punishment points to the divine decree in Surah as-Sajdah meaning \"I will surely fill Hell with jinn and humans together.\" This judgment is for those who remain obstinate after the arrival of a prophet.\n\nIt is described that shackles (aghlal) are placed around the necks of those who will not believe, reaching up to their chins, and that barriers are set before them and behind them. According to most scholars, these expressions are metaphorical; they depict a person becoming a captive to habits of disbelief and sin, unable to perceive the truth. Because of these barriers, they do not believe even if they are warned.\n\nThe one who will benefit from the warning is he who follows the Qur'an and fears the Most Merciful in the unseen (ghayb)—that is, who reveres Allah with sincere devotion. For such people, there is great forgiveness and an honorable reward. Allah declares that He will resurrect the dead, that He records the traces of deeds of good and evil left behind, and that everything is recorded in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz). This is the fundamental wisdom behind warning and giving glad tidings.\n\nAllah gives the people of a city (Antioch) as an example to the Prophet. Two prophets were sent there first, and then a third was sent to reinforce them, but the people denied them and accused them of bringing an ill omen. The prophets stated that they were only obligated to convey the message clearly. From the farthest part of the city, a man (Habib an-Najjar) came running and called upon his people to follow the messengers.\n\nThis man emphasized that the prophets ask for no payment and that they are rightly guided. He stated that he must worship Allah who created him, and that taking deities besides Him is clear misguidance. He declared, \"Indeed, I have believed in your Lord, so listen to me,\" and was martyred. Upon entering Paradise, he wished that his people could only know of this bliss.\n\nFollowing that man, Allah did not send down an army from the heavens upon the people; a single blast (sayhah) destroyed them. This shows the ultimate end of disbelieving societies. Alas for the servants! There did not come to them a prophet except that they mocked him. How many generations before them have been destroyed, and all of them will be brought together before Him for reckoning.\n\nAlmighty Allah demonstrates His power by reviving the dead earth and producing from it various plants, grains, date-palm groves, and grapevines. People eat of these fruits, yet they forget that these are Allah's bestowal and fail to show gratitude. Gratitude consists of acknowledging the blessing with the tongue as coming from Allah, believing this in the heart, and obeying Allah with the physical limbs.\n\nThe alternation of night and day, the sun running its course in a determined orbit, and the moon passing through phases until it returns like an old dried date stalk are proofs of Allah's power. It is not for the sun to overtake the moon, nor does the night outstrip the day; each floats in an orbit.\n\nJust as Allah carried mankind in Noah's ark, He also protects them in the mother's womb. Had He willed, He could have drowned them, but out of His mercy He grants them life for an appointed time. Despite this, when the disbelievers are told to fear the punishment before them and behind them, they turn away; when asked to spend on the poor, they scoff and ask, 'When will this promise be?' They await nothing but a single cry that will seize them while they are still disputing, unable even to make a bequest.\n\nWhen the Horn is blown, they will emerge from their graves and hasten to their Lord, saying, 'Woe to us! Who has raised us? This is what the Most Merciful promised, and the messengers spoke the truth.' With a single cry, all are gathered. On that Day, no soul will be wronged in the least, and everyone will be recompensed for what they did. The people of Paradise, together with their spouses, recline on couches in the shade; fruits and whatever they ask for belong to them. A greeting of 'Peace' is conveyed to them as a word from a Merciful Lord. To the criminals, however, it is said: 'Stand apart today,' and they are reminded not to worship Satan.\n\nHad Allah willed, He could have blinded the eyes of the disbelievers or transfixed them in their places; however, He has deferred their punishment to the Hereafter. He causes the human being to decline in strength with old age, which demonstrates His power. Poetry was not taught to the Prophet; the Qur'an is an admonition and a clear book. Forgetting that he was created from a drop of fluid, man asks who will give life to decayed bones. Allah declares that the One who brought them into being the first time will revive them, that He produces fire from the green tree, and that the One who created the heavens and the earth is capable of creating their likeness. When He wills a thing to be, He merely says to it, 'Be!' and it is. Exalted is the glory of Allah, in whose hand is the dominion over all things, and to Him you will all be returned.\n\nThe Qur'an is nothing other than a reminder (dhikr), an admonition and guidance from Allah, and a clear book. It is the Word of Allah to be recited in acts of worship and in places of worship.\n\nIn verse 70, this warning is given to warn and awaken those who are alive, and so that the decree of punishment may prove true against those who persist in disbelief. Just as happened to many before them, the decree of punishment has become binding upon them as well.\n\nIn verses 71–76, a reality that the heedless fail to perceive is described: We created livestock for them from what Our hands have made—that is, from blessings We created directly Ourselves. There are many subtleties in this reminder, which the discerning taste of those who read it will appreciate.\n\nThe occasion of revelation for verses 77–79 is as follows: Ubayy ibn Khalaf brought a decayed bone to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and asked, 'Can Allah bring this to life?' The Prophet (peace be upon him) replied, 'Yes, He will resurrect you as well and cast you into the Fire.' This verse emphasizes that man fails to consider that We created him from a drop of fluid, and that Allah is All-Knowing of every manner of creation.\n\nIn verse 80, it is described that Allah made fire for you from the green tree. The well-known examples are the markh and 'afar trees; while still green, they produce fire through friction. Commentators of tafsir have pointed out that what is meant here is not the wood of the tree, but the heat generated by friction, which also alludes to electricity.\n\nVerses 81–82: Is not Allah, who created the heavens and the earth, able to create the like of them? Indeed, He is capable. His command, when He intends a thing, is only to say to it, 'Be!' and it is.\n\nVerse 83: Glorify Allah, in whose hand is the dominion over all things. To Him you shall all be returned. Those who return with faith and willingly, like Habib an-Najjar, are forgiven; those who are returned by force suffer their punishment. It is narrated from Ibn Abbas that the virtues of Surah Ya-Sin were revealed on account of this verse."